// Winter, when you can see how a tree is built.
//
//     import 'trees' { Elm, Oak, Birch }
//
// A tree in leaf is mostly canopy: the wood is glimpsed between handfuls of green and its shape is
// taken on trust.  Strip the leaves and the shape is all there is, which is why this scene is set in
// the one season the library draws bare.
//
// **A branch is not a stick.**  It leaves its parent going one way and arrives at its tip going
// another, sweeping between the two, and a tree built from straight pieces reads as scaffolding no
// matter how carefully the pieces are angled.  Every limb here is a single quadratic -- one surface,
// exactly as a straight one was -- and the taper follows the bend without being asked.
//
// **The arithmetic of that is worth knowing, because getting it wrong looks like getting it right.**
// A quadratic's greatest departure from the straight line between its ends is `bend * (2k - 1) / 4`,
// where `k` says how far across that line its control point sits.  Put the control near the middle
// of the chord and the term vanishes: the tip is displaced, so the numbers all look correct, but the
// path between the ends is dead straight and the branch is still a stick pointing slightly off.  The
// first attempt at this scene's trees did exactly that and rendered as though nothing had changed.
// Here the control stands directly above each limb's base, so a limb leaves going straight up and
// leans over to reach its tip -- which is what a branch does.
//
// **How far each species sweeps is part of what it is.**  An oak is gnarled and bends most; a birch
// is a straighter thing; a fir hardly bends at all, since a conifer's leader and boughs run true.
// The direction is drawn separately from the amount, and the amount never reaches nought -- a limb
// with no bend in it is a straight line, and a curve that is really a line used to render as nothing
// at all.
//
// You will need the library, which is a thing to ask for once:
//
//     dotnet run -- libraries --install

import 'trees' { Elm, Oak, Birch }

context {
    parallel pixel scanner
    angles are degrees
    info {
        title 'Bare Wood'
        author 'J. Stephen Kress'
        description 'Winter trees, where the shape of the wood is all there is to look at.'
        copyright true
        software __software__
    }
}

camera {
    location [7.5, 5.2, -17]
    look at [-0.5, 4.6, 1]
    field of view 50
}

// A low sun almost behind them, so the limbs are drawn as edges rather than filled in -- which is
// what makes a curve read as a curve.
background physical sky {
    sun elevation 11
    sun azimuth 24
    turbidity 2.4
    brightness 3
}

sky light { samples 22 }

plane {
    material {
        pigment [0.55, 0.57, 0.60]
        ambient 0.16
        specular 0.03
    }
}

object Elm(9, 'winter', 4)    { translate [-5.6, 0, 2.5] }
object Oak(8, 'winter', 9)    { translate [0.4, 0, 0] }
object Birch(7.5, 'winter', 7) { translate [5.2, 0, -1.5] }
object Elm(7, 'winter', 12)   { translate [-10, 0, 6.5] }
